The source claims that the original model was "unreliable" because its replies differed on two runs of the same request, and that a larger model was swapped in but showed a similar level of variation. However, this claim is not well‑supported by the evidence. The evidence only shows a single pair of runs for each model ([dcd587efbf453c90] "A team measured a model's replies twice on the same request and got two different forms.") and that the researchers concluded the model was unreliable ([6cdf622a4954238e] "They concluded the model was unreliable, and replaced it with a larger one from the same family.") while the larger model also varied ([fc02e2466a9b705d] "The larger model's replies also differed between two calls on the same request, by about the same amount."). Two observations are insufficient to demonstrate systematic unreliability: language models are stochastic and can produce different outputs on repeated calls due to sampling randomness, temperature settings, or nondeterministic hardware. A proper reliability assessment would require many repetitions, control of generation parameters, and statistical analysis of variance. Without such rigor, the claim that the model is fundamentally unreliable is premature and may be wrong.

